# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your Firebase project's values.
# Find these in the Firebase console under Project Settings → General →
# "Your apps" → SDK setup and configuration.
#
# NOTE: `.env` is gitignored. Never commit real credentials.

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# Set to "true" to run against in-memory mock services instead of real
# Firebase. Useful for local UI development without a Firebase project.
# VITE_MOCK_BACKEND=true

# --- Local Firebase Emulator (FND-009; docs/testing.md "Browser E2E suite
# against the Firebase Emulator") -------------------------------------------
# Points the *real* Firebase SDK at a local Firestore/Auth emulator instead of
# either production or the in-memory mock above -- distinct from
# VITE_MOCK_BACKEND, and what proves persistence across a real page reload.
# `bun run test:browser:emulator` sets both automatically from the running
# emulator; only set these by hand to point a manual `bun run dev` at an
# emulator you started yourself (`firebase emulators:start --only
# firestore,auth --project demo-solid-groove`). Never set in a deployment.
# VITE_FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:8080
# VITE_AUTH_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9099

# --- Starter sound library (bun run library:upload) --------------------------
# Publishing the factory library to Cloud Storage needs an admin credential.
# These are for the ingestion scripts only; none of them reaches the client
# bundle, and the service account belongs in CI secrets rather than on a
# developer machine.
#
# Bucket to publish to. Falls back to VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET above.
# FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-project.firebasestorage.app
#
# Credentials — set exactly one:
#   a path to a service account JSON file, or
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/service-account.json
#   the same JSON inline, which is the form a CI secret takes.
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT={"type":"service_account",...}
#
# Point at a running Storage emulator to exercise upload without a real
# project: `firebase emulators:start --only storage`.
# FIREBASE_STORAGE_EMULATOR_HOST=127.0.0.1:9199

# --- Deployment (bun run deploy; see docs/testing.md "Deploy") --------------
# The alpha's only hosted environment is the production Firebase project
# (PRD OPS-01) -- there is no separate staging project. Deployment runs from
# CI, never from a developer machine, so none of these belong in a real local
# `.env`; they are documented here only so the values CI needs are named in
# one place. Set them on the `prod` GitHub *environment* on
# `afternoon/solid-groove` (Settings -> Environments -> prod), which is the
# environment .github/workflows/ci.yml's `deploy` job declares:
#
#   FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID (environment *variable*, not secret -- a project ID
#     is not sensitive; consumed by the deploy step and the hosted smoke
#     test's URL)
#   FIREBASE_DEPLOY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT (environment *secret* -- inline service
#     account JSON with Firebase Hosting, Firestore rules/indexes, and
#     Storage rules deploy permissions; CI writes it to a temp file and
#     points GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS at it for a non-interactive
#     `firebase deploy`)
#
# Scope matters: a job only receives an environment's values if it declares
# that environment, and an environment-scoped `vars.*` read from a job that
# does not resolves to the empty string with no error. Setting these at
# repository scope instead leaves the `deploy` job reading blanks.

# --- Analytics and error monitoring (FND-001c; PRD OPS-02/OPS-03, ADR 0001) --
# Two processors: Google Analytics 4 for product events, which rides the
# Firebase config above (VITE_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID -- nothing extra to set),
# and Sentry for error monitoring.
#
# PUBLIC-BY-DESIGN, ships in the client bundle, not a secret:
#
#   VITE_SENTRY_DSN
#     The DSN identifies a Sentry project's ingest endpoint and grants nothing
#     but the ability to submit events to it -- exactly like the Firebase Web
#     API key above. It is *required* to be public: a browser SDK cannot send
#     an event without it. Set it as a GitHub Actions repository *variable* so
#     it is visible in logs and config like any other build input. With it
#     unset, src/monitoring/sentrySink.ts's `start()` resolves false and the
#     app runs with error reporting disabled rather than failing.
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN=
#
# CI-ONLY SECRET, must never reach a client bundle or a developer machine:
#
#   SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN (repository *secret*)
#     Grants release creation and source-map upload for the org. This is the
#     credential that would let someone rewrite what your error data says, so
#     unlike the DSN it is a real secret. scripts/verify-no-secrets-in-bundle.mjs
#     fails the build if it ever appears in the built output. Scope it to
#     `project:releases` (and `org:read`) for the one project below.
#
# CI configuration (repository *variables*, not sensitive):
#
#   SENTRY_ORG      the Sentry organization slug
#   SENTRY_PROJECT  the Sentry project slug
#
# All four are read only by CI's `deploy` job (.github/workflows/ci.yml). With
# any of SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN / SENTRY_ORG / SENTRY_PROJECT unset, app.config.ts's
# build plugin is inactive: the deploy still succeeds, source maps are still
# generated and still withheld from Hosting, they are just not uploaded, so
# stack traces in Sentry stay minified. Nothing here belongs in a real local
# `.env` -- a local build needs none of it. See docs/testing.md, "Verifying
# analytics and errors against a deployed build".

# Stamped automatically into the client by app.config.ts: CI's `deploy` job
# sets this to the exact commit it built and is deploying; a local build
# falls back to `git rev-parse HEAD` and needs nothing set here. See
# src/release.ts.
# VITE_RELEASE_SHA=
